227	## Controller amendments (2026-09-14 21:35) — these override the task text above where they differ
228	
229	### A. The socket is a state the streamer enters, not a consequence of configuration
230	
231	The "When the plugin connects" rule above is **withdrawn**. A Stream Deck is never off, so "connected whenever configured" would hold a socket open around the clock for nothing. Instead:
232	
233	- A **sixth action, `Connect`**, UUID `com.scrthq.runlog.connect`, `Controllers: ["Keypad"]`, tooltip `Connects the deck to your Runlog account, and disconnects it again.`, placeholder icon, inspector `ui/connect.html` (the shared sign-in fragment plus nothing else). Add it to the manifest and register it in `plugin.ts`.
234	- `DeckState` gains `on: boolean` and `idleOff: boolean`; `DeckEvent` gains `{ t: "on"; on: boolean; idle?: boolean }` — `on: true` clears `idleOff`; `on: false` with `idle: true` sets it. `reduce` on `on: false` also drops `socket` to `"closed"`, `runs` to `[]`, `attached`/`snapshot` to null (the pin survives).
235	- `common()` gains, immediately after the session checks: `if (!state.on) return { title: "Not connected", tone: "dim", when: "press Connect" }`. Every existing face reads that while off — the state on top, the thing to do beneath it (user 21:35: a key that depends on the connection should say to make it first).
236	- A new `connectFace(state): Face`: session none → "Sign in"; session expired → "Sign in again"; `!on && idleOff` → "Idle · press to connect"; `!on` → "Offline"; `socket === "connecting"` → "Connecting"; open and attached → the run's name (`when`: its packTitle); open and not attached → whatever `common()` says past the `on` check ("No run open" / "Not synced" / "Pick a run"). Tests for each in `state.test.ts` (extend Task 6's file; it is in your touch list now).
237	- `plugin.ts`: no `ensureConnected`. Two functions: `turnOn()` — dispatch `on: true`, resolve the account (below), `wire.connect()`; `turnOff(idle = false)` — `wire.disconnect()`, dispatch `{ t: "on", on: false, idle }`. On launch the plugin is **off**: no socket, no token refresh. `Connect`'s `onKeyDown` toggles: off → `turnOn()`; on → `turnOff()`. While on, `openWire`'s own reconnect-through-drops stays as Task 9 built it.
238	- **Idle auto-off**: a store subscription keeps a 30-minute timer that starts whenever `on && socket === "open" && runs.length === 0` and is cleared whenever `runs.length > 0` or the plugin goes off; when it fires, `turnOff(true)`. Constant `IDLE_OFF_MS = 30 * 60_000`. Test the rule as a pure helper (`idleDeadline(state, now, since)` or similar) rather than through real timers.
239	- `willAppear` still marks nothing about connecting; it only draws. The `configured` global setting from the task text above is not needed and is not written.
240	
241	### B. The address is a setting, not an environment variable
242	
243	- Global settings gain `apiBase: string` (default `https://runlog.scrthq.com`). The shared sign-in fragment `ui/signin.html` gains, above **Sign in**, an `<sdpi-textfield>` "Runlog address" bound to that global setting (sdpi-components binds global settings with `setting="…"` plus `global` — read the sdpi-components v4 docs in `node_modules` or its README for the exact attribute; if it cannot bind a global directly, post `{ t: "address", apiBase }` to the plugin and have the plugin save it).
244	- `plugin.ts` drops `process.env["RUNLOG_API"]`. `openWire` (Task 9) takes its `Account` **as a getter** — change its first parameter to `account: Account | (() => Account)` and resolve it inside `open()` on every connect — so the address read at `turnOn` is the one used. The module-level `wire` in `plugin.ts` passes a getter that returns the cached global setting (cache it from `getGlobalSettings()` at launch and on every `didReceiveGlobalSettings`).
245	- The `wss://` base is derived from `apiBase` (`replace(/^http/, "ws")`), as Task 9 already does.
246	
247	### C. The inspector's account block
248	
249	`ui/signin.html` (Task 8's fragment) is extended and wired on the plugin side:
250	- **Sign in** → `{ t: "signin" }` → the plugin runs `signIn(account, say, open)`; `say` lines from the device flow are exactly `"  Open  <url>"` and `"  Code  <code>"` (two leading spaces, two spaces after the word) — parse those two, post `{ t: "code", code, url }` to the inspector; on completion post `{ t: "who", name: "you", signedIn: true }` and, if the deck is off, leave it off (the streamer presses Connect).
251	- **Sign out** → `{ t: "signout" }` → `turnOff()`, `signOut()`, dispatch `session: "none"`, post `{ t: "who", signedIn: false }`.
252	- **Reconnect** → `{ t: "reconnect" }` → `turnOff()` then `turnOn()`.
253	- On `propertyInspectorDidAppear`, post `{ t: "who", signedIn: loadSession() !== null, apiBase }` so the block shows the current state.
254	
255	### D. Everything else in the task text stands
256	
257	`RunlogAction`, `draw`, `send`, the four action classes, the `press.ts` inspector receiving `{ t: "offer", moves, presets }`, the hardware check — as written, with `Connect` added to the list and "Not connected / press Connect" as the resting face after install. The hardware sequence becomes: **Sign in** (inspector) → keys read *Not connected · press Connect* → press Connect → *Connecting* → *No run open* → open a run in the browser → the follow key names the primary within a second → press it → the browser moves, the key shows the OK tick → press Connect again → every key reads *Not connected · press Connect*.
258	
259	Touch list, widened: `streamdeck/src/plugin.ts`, `streamdeck/src/state.ts`, `streamdeck/src/state.test.ts`, `streamdeck/src/socket.ts` (the getter only), `streamdeck/src/socket.test.ts` (if the getter needs a test), `streamdeck/src/actions/**`, `streamdeck/com.scrthq.runlog.sdPlugin/manifest.json`, `streamdeck/com.scrthq.runlog.sdPlugin/ui/**`, `streamdeck/com.scrthq.runlog.sdPlugin/imgs/actions/connect/**` (placeholder copies).
